    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **James Wood**, who commands the highest efficiency score of the day (74.75). Facing a favorable matchup against a left-handed arm with an xERA of 4.07, **James Wood** benefits from a massive 14% park factor boost and an elite 0.430 xwOBA projection, cementing his role as our primary offensive engine out of the leadoff spot. We are also leaning heavily into the matchup against Bratt, whose 7.04 xERA makes **Tyler Soderstrom** (70.82) a must-start; despite a minor platoon penalty, the massive SP skill differential drives his efficiency into our \"Strong\" tier. **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) also provides significant value, capitalizing on a favorable right-handed matchup against Junk to serve as our anchor at third base.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Austin Riley**, who posted a strong 63.87, narrowly missing the starting cut behind the higher-scoring **Josh Naylor** (64.04). While **Austin Riley** remains a high-floor asset, the optimizer favored **Josh Naylor**\u2019s specific situational alignment for the day. Meanwhile, we continue to exercise caution with speed-specialists like **Jose Caballero** and **Travis Bazzana**; despite their importance in our pursuit of the stolen base category, both fell below our 40.0 efficiency floor due to challenging pitching matchups\u2014specifically facing xERAs near or below 3.30\u2014necessitating a benching to preserve our game caps. We are proceeding with **Gabriel Moreno**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Cole Young**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Brice Turang**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Henry Bolte** as currently configured, with no major weather-related warnings impacting the primary slate. We remain prepared to pivot if late lineup changes affect **Zach McKinstry** or **Henry Bolte** in the bottom of the order.",
